# Findings `make trivy-config` is allowed to skip, each with the reason
# it is skipped. This file exists so that scan can be a BLOCKING check
# rather than an advisory one: with the two known findings written down,
# anything new is a real failure instead of noise somebody learns to
# scroll past.
#
# Adding an entry here is a decision, not a formality -- it needs a
# reason a reviewer can disagree with. Delete an entry to see the
# finding again.

# KSV-0014: "Root file system is not read-only", on the trivy/grype DB
# primer Jobs and refresh CronJobs.
#
# Deliberate, and already documented at each site: those pods run
# trivy/grype/psql, which write to TMPDIR and other rootfs paths that
# have never been enumerated here. This is not an oversight -- assuming
# a read-only rootfs is exactly what crash-looped monitor-api the last
# time it was added without checking (see the securityContext comments
# in templates/postgres/backup-cronjob.yaml and its neighbours).
#
# The real fix is per-Job: readOnlyRootFilesystem: true plus an emptyDir
# at every path the tool actually writes to, verified by running it.
# That is a chart change with a real chance of breaking the
# vulnerability-DB pipeline, so it is not being smuggled into a CI
# hardening change.
KSV-0014

# KSV-0109: "ConfigMap with secrets", on monitor-api-config.
#
# False positive. It matches the KEY NAME
# REGISTRY_CREDENTIALS_SECRET, whose value is the NAME of a Secret
# ("scm-registry-credentials") that monitor-api then reads -- it is a
# pointer to a credential, not a credential. Every actual secret in this
# chart lives in a Secret and is injected via secretKeyRef; see
# templates/monitor-api/deployment.yaml.
KSV-0109
